<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Oldenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1946 CE · capital Oldenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North-west German republic that succeeded the grand duchy and in 1932 elected one of the first Nazi state governments.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-ii-grand-duke-of-oldenburg">Grand Duke Frederick Augustus II</a> abdicated in November 1918, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> became a free state, with a constitution adopted in 1919. It kept the detached principalities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Lübeck</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eutin">Eutin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkenfeld">Birkenfeld</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> was rural, with dairy farming in the marshes and moors and a naval port at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmshaven">Wilhelmshaven</a> nearby. Its Lutheran north and Catholic south around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloppenburg">Cloppenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vechta">Vechta</a> differed politically. In 1932 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazis</a> won an absolute majority, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-rover">Carl Röver</a> formed one of the first Nazi state governments. In 1937 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-hamburg-act">Greater Hamburg Act</a> transferred <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkenfeld">Birkenfeld</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and gave Wilhelmshaven to Oldenburg.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-rover">Röver</a> became Reich governor for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-hanseatic-city-of-bremen">Bremen</a>. After 1945 the state lay in the British zone, and in November 1946 it was merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schaumburg-lippe">Schaumburg-Lippe</a> into the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>. Oldenburg survives as a historic region and administrative name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-oldenburg.jpg" alt="Free State of Oldenburg" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Oldenburg_(St._George_Cross).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oldenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carl Röver</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Freistaat Oldenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=free-state-of-oldenburg" aria-label="Free State of Oldenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4863 348 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Oldenburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5183 604L5183 604M5183 604L5300 622M5183 604L5300 622M5183 604L5165 704"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5183 604h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0M5165 704h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5183 604h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5183 604h0"/></svg></a>